---
import { BLIPFOTO_SUPPORT_REQUEST } from '../blipfoto-links';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StatusDisclaimer from '../components/StatusDisclaimer.astro';

// Every statement about what the monitor does here describes packages/b-status as built
// (probe, retry, snapshot, user reports). Re-check this page whenever that changes (b-oss#493).
const sections = [
  ['what', 'What we check'],
  ['often', 'How often'],
  ['states', 'Up, Down and Unknown'],
  ['charts', 'Reading the charts'],
  ['reports', 'User reports'],
  ['uptime', 'Uptime'],
  ['times', 'Times and days'],
  ['privacy', 'Privacy'],
] as const;
---

<Layout
  title="How the Blipfoto status page works (unofficial) · b-oss"
  description="What the unofficial Blipfoto status page checks, how often, and how to read it."
  nav="status"
>
  <StatusDisclaimer slot="below-header" />

  <div class="status-page hw-page">
    <header class="st-head">
      <h1>How this works</h1>
      <a class="st-howlink" href="status.html">&lsaquo; Back to status</a>
    </header>

    <div class="hw-layout">
      <nav class="hw-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="st-eyebrow">On this page</p>
        <ul>
          {sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <div class="hw-body">
        <section id="what" aria-labelledby="hw-what">
          <h2 id="hw-what">What we check</h2>
          <p>
            Two parts of Blipfoto, separately, because they can fail separately. Each check is a simple,
            read-only request; we don’t log in.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Site</strong> is blipfoto.com in a web browser. We load the browse page, then one
              of its entries, then that entry’s photo.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>API</strong> is what apps such as b-ark and b-mobile use. We make the same kind of
              requests they do, using an app registration made for this monitor, and check for a proper
              answer rather than an error.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Photos matter to both, so if photos don’t load, Site and API both show as Down: one cause,
            not two. A check also fails if the answer is far too slow to be usable. When one fails we
            retry a few seconds later, and only a second failure counts.
          </p>
          <p>
            All checks run from one place, outside Blipfoto’s network. A fault that only affects some
            places, or only your own connection, may not show here.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="often" aria-labelledby="hw-often">
          <h2 id="hw-often">How often</h2>
          <p>
            Every 15 minutes. The page shows when it last checked and when the next check is due, and
            never refreshes itself, so nothing moves while you read. Because we only look every 15
            minutes, a fault shorter than that may not show, and start times, end times and durations
            are approximate, to the nearest 15 minutes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="states" aria-labelledby="hw-states">
          <h2 id="hw-states">Up, Down and Unknown</h2>
          <dl class="hw-states">
            <dt><span class="st-badge st-badge-up"><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Up</span></dt>
            <dd>Our last check worked.</dd>
            <dt><span class="st-badge st-badge-down"><span aria-hidden="true">✕</span> Down</span></dt>
            <dd>Our last check failed, and so did the retry. An <strong>incident</strong> is a period spent Down.</dd>
            <dt><span class="st-badge st-badge-unknown"><span aria-hidden="true">?</span> Unknown</span></dt>
            <dd>
              We have no recent data. That’s on our side, not necessarily Blipfoto’s, and it counts as
              neither Up nor Down.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="charts" aria-labelledby="hw-charts">
          <h2 id="hw-charts">Reading the charts</h2>
          <p>Charts show only downtime, so an empty lane is good news.</p>
          <ul class="hw-key">
            <li><span class="st-key st-key-down" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Red:</strong> Down. In the 30-day chart, a taller bar means more downtime that day.</span></li>
            <li><span class="st-key st-key-nodata" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Striped grey:</strong> no data. We couldn’t check then.</span></li>
            <li><span class="st-key st-key-shared" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Amber:</strong> user reports.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>A bar with a white cut</strong> was down for over 4 hours. We cut long bars short so short ones stay visible; the real time is printed above.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>Select a mark for details. On a phone, use the Earlier and Later buttons under each chart.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="reports" aria-labelledby="hw-reports">
          <h2 id="hw-reports">User reports</h2>
          <p>
            Anyone can share a problem from the status page. Each one is a <strong>user report</strong>,
            counted for the Site (“the website”) or the API (“an app”) and drawn as amber bars. They are
            not verified and never change the uptime figures. Sometimes people report something our
            checks don’t see, such as one feature failing or a fault that only affects some people.
          </p>
          <div class="hw-callout" role="note">
            <strong>Reports stay on this page.</strong> They are not sent to Blipfoto and we can’t fix
            Blipfoto. To get something fixed, <a href={BLIPFOTO_SUPPORT_REQUEST}>contact Blipfoto Support</a>.
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="uptime" aria-labelledby="hw-uptime">
          <h2 id="hw-uptime">Uptime</h2>
          <p>
            The share of checked time that each part was Up; time we couldn’t check is left out. We
            always round down: 100% means no downtime at all, and very high figures show extra decimals
            (such as 99.999%) so they are never rounded up to 100%.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="times" aria-labelledby="hw-times">
          <h2 id="hw-times">Times and days</h2>
          <p>
            Times of day are UK time. The 30-day chart and the uptime figures count each day from
            midnight to midnight UTC, an hour behind UK clocks in summer, so downtime just after
            midnight UK time can appear on the previous day.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="privacy" aria-labelledby="hw-privacy">
          <h2 id="hw-privacy">Privacy</h2>
          <p>
            No account is needed to share a report. We keep your answers and the time, and nothing else
            about you. To keep the counts fair we limit reports from one place, using an anonymous
            marker (a one-way scramble of your connection’s address) kept for about half an hour, then
            deleted. Reporting uses Cloudflare’s Turnstile anti-bot check. The code for this page and the
            monitor is open source: <a href="https://github.com/IanMStevenson/b-oss">see the source</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
